CHAPTER VI

SPECIAL PROTECTION FOR CHILDREN

Article 38

The Regional Government and other institutions are obliged and  responsible to provide Special Protection  to Children.

Special Protection for Children as referred to  in paragraph (1), is provided to:
a. Children in Emergency Situations;
b. Children in Conflict with the Law;
c. Children from Minority and Isolated Groups;
d. Children who are Exploited Economically and/or  Sexually;
e. Children who are Victims of Narcotics, Alcohol, Psychotropic, and other Addictive Substance Abuse, . Children who are Victims of Pornography;
g. Children with HIV and AIDS;
h. Child Victims of Kidnapping, Sale, and/or  Trafficking;

Child Victims of Physical and/or Psychological Violence;  l s Children with Disabilities, Child Victims of Sexual Crimes;

Child Victims of Terrorism Networks;

2e Children who are Victims of Stigmatization from Labeling . Child Victims of Mistreatment and Neglect;  Children with Deviant Social Behavior, and Related to the Condition of Their Parents.

3. Special Protection for Children is carried out through efforts to:  a. rapid handling, including treatment  and/or physical, psychological, and social  rehabilitation, as well as prevention of diseases and other health disorders;
b. psychosocial support during treatment  until recovery;
c. provision of social assistance for Children from underprivileged families; dand. provision of protection and accompaniment in every judicial process.
4. Special Protection for Children as referred to  in paragraph (1) is provided at UPTD PPA that has been established  with reference to established service standards.
5. Special Protection for Children as referred to  in paragraph (1) is implemented quickly, comprehensively and  in an integrated manner.

Article 39

1. Special Protection for Children in Emergency Situations as referred to in Article 38 paragraph (2) letter a, shall be provided to:
a. Children who are refugees;
b. Children victims of riots;
c. Children victims of natural disasters; and
d. Children in armed conflict situations.
2. In addition to children as referred to in paragraph (1), Special Protection for Children in Emergency Situations shall also be provided to:
a. Children victims of social disasters;
b. Children victims of non-natural disasters; and
c. Children of female convicts/detainees.
3. Special Protection as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out through:
a. prevention so that children do not become victims in emergency situations;
collecting data on the number of children requiring Special Protection in emergency situations;
mapping the basic and specific needs of children requiring Special Protection in emergency situations;
guarantee of security and safety of children in emergency situations;
data collection of children and their families for tracing and family reunification;
priority emergency rescue, evacuation, and security measures;
recovery of physical, psychological and social health of children in emergency situations;
provision of legal assistance, accompaniment, physical, psychological and social rehabilitation of children in emergency situations;
care;
improvement of facilities needed by children in emergency situations;
fulfillment of basic and specific needs of children consisting of food, clothing, shelter, Education, provision of health services, learning and recreation, security guarantees, and equal treatment;
fulfillment of special needs for children with disabilities and children experiencing psychosocial problems;
exemption from Education fees whether conducted in formal or non-formal Educational Institutions during the emergency period;
provision of services for the fulfillment of children's identity rights and important documents lost due to emergency situations; and/or provision of social reintegration services.
4. Special Protection for Children as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be provided in the form of care, upbringing, as well as fulfillment of basic needs and special needs of children in accordance with their age level and development.

Article 40

Special Protection for children in Conflict with the Law as referred to in Article 38 Paragraph (2) letter b, shall be carried out through:

a. humane treatment with attention to needs according to their age, separation from adults;
provision of legal assistance and other assistance effectively;
implementation of recreational activities;
freedom from torture, punishment, or other treatment that is cruel, inhuman, and degrading to dignity and degree;
avoidance of imposition of death penalty and/or life imprisonment;
avoidance of arrest, detention, or imprisonment, except as a last resort and for the shortest possible time;
provision of justice before a children's court that is objective, impartial, and in a hearing closed to the public;
avoidance of publication of their identity;
provision of accompaniment by parents/guardians and persons trusted by the child;
provision of social advocacy;
provision of private life;
provision of accessibility, especially for children with disabilities;
provision of Education;
provision of health services; and provision of other rights in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

2. Special Protection for Children in Conflict with the Law as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out through efforts of:
a. prevention,
b. administrative settlement of cases;
c. rehabilitation; and
d. social reintegration.

Article 42

1. Special Protection for Children Who Are Exploited Economically and/or Sexually as referred to in Article 38 paragraph (2) letter d, shall be carried out through:
a. dissemination and/or socialization of provisions of laws and regulations related to the protection of Children Who Are Exploited Economically and/or Sexually;
b. monitoring, reporting, and imposition of sanctions; and
c. involvement of various companies, labor unions, non-governmental organizations, and the community in the elimination of exploitation of children economically and/or sexually.
2. In the event that Children Who Are Exploited Economically and/or sexually require recovery to their original condition, services must be provided in the form of:
a. medical rehabilitation;
b. social rehabilitation;
c. legal assistance and social assistance; and/or
d. repatriation and social reintegration.

Article 48

Special Protection for Child Victims of Sexual Crimes as referred to in Article 38 paragraph (2) letter j, shall be carried out through:

a. education about reproductive health, religious values, and moral values;
b. social rehabilitation;
c. psychosocial accompaniment during treatment until recovery; and
d. provision of protection and accompaniment at every level of examination starting from investigation, prosecution, up to examination in court hearings.

Article 49

1. Special Protection for Child Victims of Terrorism Networks as referred to in Article 38 paragraph (2) letter k, shall be carried out through efforts of:
a. education about education, ideology and values of nationalism;
pog rehabilitasi sosial, and counseling about the dangers of terrorism;
rehabilitation about the dangers of terrorism;
e. social accompaniment.
2. Special Protection for Child Victims of Terrorism Networks shall also be provided in the form of:
a. fulfillment of the rights of Child Victims of Terrorism Networks to care and psychological health recovery;
b. medical rehabilitation;
re-education and social reintegration; and
d. guarantee of safety both physical, mental, and social for Child Victims of Terrorism Networks.

CHAPTER VII

GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

Article 54

1. The Regent shall conduct guidance and supervision over the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence in the region.
2. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) shall include:
a. provision of implementation instructions;
b. technical guidance and training;
c. supervision;
d. monitoring; and
e. evaluation of service implementation.

Article 55

In order to achieve the objectives of protection for women (1) and children victims of violence, the Regional Government may conduct cooperation with:

a. the central government;
b. the provincial government;
c. other regency/city governments;
d. law enforcement institutions/ and/or law enforcement officers;
e. non-governmental organizations;
f. the community;
g. universities/ academics; and
h. mass media.

Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall include:  (2)

a. exchange of data and information;
b. recovery of victims of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration;
d. provision of evidence and witnesses; and
e. follow-up in accordance with applicable law.

Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (3) (2) shall be set forth in the form of a cooperation agreement in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
